# Sokoban's formatting, written down.
#
# NOT YET APPLIED. This file describes how the codebase is already written; it
# has deliberately not been run over the tree. Read the cost below before you
# do, because the decision is a repository-history decision, not a cleanup.
#
# How it was derived: twenty clang-format options were tuned against the actual
# source rather than picked from a house style, keeping whichever value moved
# the fewest lines. Measured with clang-format 18 over all 367 files under
# src/ and tests/ (104,573 lines):
#
#   stock LLVM/Google/Chromium/Mozilla   167,000 - 171,000 lines rewritten
#   stock Microsoft                       75,559
#   stock WebKit (closest of the stock)   41,488
#   this file                             24,672 lines, 296 of 367 files
#   this file with ColumnLimit: 0         10,573 lines, 210 of 367 files
#
# So: no configuration reproduces this codebase. 71 files are already exactly
# conformant, which says the *style* is consistent - braces, indentation,
# pointer alignment, initializer lists all agree. What no tool reproduces is
# where a long call gets broken. Those wrap points are hand-chosen for
# readability, and clang-format has one algorithm where a person has judgment.
#
# Three ways to adopt, in increasing cost:
#
#   1. New files only. Nothing existing moves. The tree converges as files are
#      rewritten anyway. Costs nothing and buys nothing immediately.
#   2. Format on touch: run it over a file you were already substantially
#      editing. Keeps each reformat inside a change that was going to churn
#      that file regardless.
#   3. One reformat commit. 24,672 lines, 296 files, and `git blame` points at
#      that commit for most of the repository afterwards. `git blame -w
#      --ignore-rev` and a .git-blame-ignore-revs file make that survivable,
#      but every tool that reads blame has to know about it.
#
# Setting ColumnLimit to 0 tells clang-format never to re-wrap, which more than
# halves the churn - but it still restructures 10,573 lines, because it will
# still join lines that fit. It is not a free option either.

BasedOnStyle: WebKit
Language: Cpp
Standard: c++20

ColumnLimit: 80
IndentWidth: 4
TabWidth: 4
UseTab: Never
NamespaceIndentation: None

# Parameters go one per line once a signature breaks; arguments follow the same
# rule. This is the single strongest pattern in the codebase.
BinPackParameters: false
BinPackArguments: false
AllowAllParametersOfDeclarationOnNextLine: false
AllowAllArgumentsOnNextLine: true
AlignAfterOpenBracket: AlwaysBreak

BreakBeforeBinaryOperators: None
AllowShortFunctionsOnASingleLine: Inline
AllowShortIfStatementsOnASingleLine: WithoutElse
# A return type almost never sits on its own line here; the signature breaks
# after the open bracket instead.
PenaltyReturnTypeOnItsOwnLine: 1000
